NAME
    memcard - manage memory cards, controller paks, and console backup RAM

SYNOPSIS
    memcard ls [<card>]
    memcard cp <card>:<save> <card>
    memcard mv <card>:<save> <card>
    memcard rm <card>:<save>
    memcard import <card> <from>...
    memcard new <card>
    memcard dup <card> <new card>
    memcard rename <card> <new card>

DESCRIPTION
    Manages save media across consoles that store saves on dedicated cards
    or backup RAM: PlayStation (.mcr), PlayStation 2 (.ps2), Sega CD
    internal backup RAM (.brm) and RAM cartridges (.crm), and N64
    Controller Paks (.mpk). Does not require a running game.

    Each <card> is a file path inside the shell sandbox, for example:

        /Saves/PlayStation/Cards/Card 1.mcr       shared or named card
        /Saves/PlayStation/Amber Saga.mcr         per-game card
        /Saves/PlayStation 2/Cards/Card 1.ps2     PlayStation 2 card
        /Saves/Sega CD/Console/Backup RAM 1.brm   console backup RAM

    Media types are identified case-insensitively by file extension from
    the media declared by installed engines in systems-table order. A
    <save> is identified either by its save ID on the card or by its
    1-based index from 'memcard ls <card>'.

OPTIONS
    ls [<card>]
        With no argument, list every card under /Saves grouped by console
        with its medium, free capacity, and lock status. With <card>, list
        each save on the card with its index, size, ID, and title.

    cp <card>:<save> <card>
        Copy a save onto another card of the same medium.

    mv <card>:<save> <card>
        Copy a save to the destination card, verify by reading it back,
        and only then delete it from the source card.

    rm <card>:<save>
        Delete a save using the console's native deletion semantics
        (marking directory entries deleted on PlayStation cards; removing
        the save directory and its files on PlayStation 2 cards).

    import <card> <from>...
        Copy every save from all <from> cards onto <card> atomically (all
        or none). Refuses if total space exceeds free capacity (15 blocks
        on PlayStation, 7,998 KB on formatted PlayStation 2) or if two
        sources hold conflicting saves with the same ID. Saves already on
        <card> are preserved and <from> cards are never modified, leaving
        per-game cards intact if Per Game mode is later re-selected in
        Save Media settings.

    new <card>
        Create a new card, formatted when the medium has a format module;
        otherwise create an unformatted image for the game to format on
        first save.

    dup <card> <new card>
        Duplicate an entire card image byte-for-byte (works for all media).

    rename <card> <new card>
        Rename a card file in place (works for all media).

LOCKING AND FORMATS
    A running game creates <card>.lock beside each card it holds. Any
    mutating subcommand refuses a locked card and names the holder ("in
    use by <game>" or "in use by another program"). Close the game before
    modifying its cards.

    Format modules handle save-level inspection and transfers for
    PlayStation, PlayStation 2, and Sega CD backup RAM/cartridge media.
    Media without a format module (such as N64 Controller Paks) report
    file size in 'ls' and support 'new', 'dup', and 'rename'.

EXAMPLES
    memcard ls
    cd /Saves/PlayStation
    memcard new Cards/Blue.mcr
    memcard ls "Amber Saga.mcr"
    memcard cp "Amber Saga.mcr:1" Cards/Blue.mcr
    memcard import Cards/Blue.mcr "Amber Saga.mcr" "Basalt Run.mcr"
    memcard ls "../PlayStation 2/Cards/Card 1.ps2"

EXIT STATUS
    0   Operation succeeded.
    1   Invalid arguments, unknown extension, path outside sandbox, locked
        card, insufficient space, name conflict, or I/O error.

SEE ALSO
